      },
      "lineNbr": 6973,
      "author": {
        "id": 1499410
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T21:54:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It looks like the AUX part isn\u0027t needed. `perf_mmap()` still rejects a non-zero `vm_slice_off` (aosp/4368055) and needs the AUX size in whole kernel pages, so an AUX VMA starts at slice 0 of a kernel page. The native loop below already maps that: since aosp/4368055, `remap_pfn_range()` of a kernel page maps all four of its slices in a 4KB VMA, so `perf_map_aux_ppps()` ends up with the same PTEs.\n\nFor the same reason, `vma_file_offset(vma)` is always `vm_pgoff \u003c\u003c PAGE_SHIFT` for these VMAs, so `aux_offset`, `perf_aux_offset()` and `perf_mmap_is_aux()` give the same answers as the `vm_pgoff` checks they replace.\n\nWould it be OK to drop them, and only send the main ring to the 4KB path here, e.g. `if (mm_is_p3s_4k(vma-\u003evm_mm) \u0026\u0026 !vma-\u003evm_pgoff)`?\n\nNote that `tools/perf` puts the AUX area right after the ring, at (1 + 2^n) × 4KB in a 4KB process, so it would still fail the slice check. If AUX matters in 4KB processes, I think that needs its own change.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "fcddcede99bd82769c30134512e2e92f7d3170f8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "44892b5c_356daa56",
        "filename": "kernel/events/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 7087,
      "author": {
        "id": 1499410
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T21:54:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this changes the accounting for x86_64 page size emulation. There the metadata area is `__PAGE_SIZE / PAGE_SIZE` kernel pages (4 for 16KB), and `user_extra` has counted all of them since ACK\u0027s \"ANDROID: 16K: Fixup perf_mmap check for metadata page\" (9bb7e0640feb); with this change it counts one.\n\nThat matches the data + 1 pages that `perf_mmap_close()` subtracts, so it may well be the right number, but it\u0027s an x86_64 change rather than a P3S one. Would you mind keeping `nr_pages + __PAGE_SIZE / PAGE_SIZE` here, which is `nr_pages + 1` on arm64, and fixing the imbalance in a separate patch if needed?",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "fcddcede99bd82769c30134512e2e92f7d3170f8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a67a0ba6_ca4803c3",
        "filename": "kernel/events/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 7103,
      "author": {
        "id": 1499410
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T21:54:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m worried this breaks repeat mappings of a ring buffer, and every AUX mapping, under x86_64 page size emulation. Without `CONFIG_ARM64_PER_PROCESS_PAGE_SIZE`, `perf_mmap_page_size()` returns `__PAGE_SIZE`, the emulated 16KB, while `mm_pte_size()` is the 4KB kernel page. So this check, and the one at the top of `perf_mmap_aux()`, return `-EINVAL` there.\n\nCould the stored and the compared values come from one helper, which `metadata_size` in `perf_mmap()` and `perf_set_mmap_page_size()` would use too? Something like:\n\n```\nstatic unsigned long perf_mm_page_size(struct mm_struct *mm)\n{\n        return mm_is_p3s_4k(mm) ? mm_pte_size(mm) : __PAGE_SIZE;\n}\n```",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "fcddcede99bd82769c30134512e2e92f7d3170f8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2c219af8_10ab6eed",
        "filename": "kernel/events/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 7273,
      "author": {
        "id": 1499410
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T21:54:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Would it be OK to refuse `mmap()` of perf events in 4KB processes with `p3s_4k_unsupported()` instead, as I asked for BPF maps on aosp/4367949 and aosp/4367950?\n\nSince aosp/4368055, a 4KB process can already map a ring buffer laid out in 16KB pages: `perf_mmap()` rejects a non-zero `vm_slice_off`, `map_range()` maps whole kernel pages, and the user page reports a `data_offset` of 16KB. Sizes that aren\u0027t a multiple of 16KB, such as (1 + 2^n) × 4KB, fail with `-EINVAL`.\n\nI may be missing a use case: simpleperf and Perfetto\u0027s traced_perf are native binaries, and simpleperf normally starts from the shell, also for `--app`. The case I can think of is a 4KB app that starts simpleperf itself (e.g. through simpleperf\u0027s `app_api`), since `ADDR_PAGE_SIZE_4KB` survives exec. Is that something P3S needs to support?\n\nThe 4KB layout costs explicit-slice PTEs from a new mapper, sub-page rings in the output path of every ring buffer, and about 200 added lines in `kernel/events/`. A refusal would be a few lines next to the `vma_slice_off()` check, `perf_event_open()` and `read()` would keep working, and `remap_pfn_range_slice()` could move to aosp/4368009, its other user.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "fcddcede99bd82769c30134512e2e92f7d3170f8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "67bea9d1_42ddb17f",
        "filename": "kernel/events/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 15
      },
      "lineNbr": 9732,
      "author": {
        "id": 1499410
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T21:54:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "These offsets look right to me, and I think they\u0027re needed whichever way the ring buffer goes. When a native simpleperf profiles a 4KB app today, `PERF_RECORD_MMAP2` reports `vm_pgoff \u003c\u003c PAGE_SHIFT` without the 4KB slice, so symbols in a library segment mapped at a 4KB offset come out wrong. The same goes for the address filters in `perf_addr_filter_vma_adjust()`, and `perf_get_pgtable_size()` walks a 4KB mm with the kernel\u0027s PMD index and page sizes.\n\nWould you mind moving these three into a separate patch, together with `perf_page_size_p3s.c` and the `PERF_RECORD_MMAP` check of `perf_mmap_offset_p3s.c` from aosp/4367986? They don\u0027t depend on the ring layout, so they could land whatever we decide for `perf_mmap()`.",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "fcddcede99bd82769c30134512e2e92f7d3170f8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
